@meta
id: 21-09
title: A macro on every line of a range
chapter: 21
type: lesson
skills: macro-play, macro-record
par: 76
minutes: 7
vscode: select lines 3 to 17 and hope multi-cursor lands on all of them
open: errors.ts

@brief
The error table for the new payments module was pasted in from the
incident sheet: a code and a status per line. Each becomes an entry:

    ORDER_NOT_FOUND 404    →    'ORDER_NOT_FOUND': { statusCode: 404, sentry: false },

Sixteen lines, from line 2 to line 17. Record on the first and let an
Ex range play the macro on the others.

`:normal` from chapter 15 is not tied to `:g`. It takes any range, and
`@a` is an ordinary normal-mode command. So `:3,17normal @a` plays the
macro on every line from 3 to 17, one line at a time, with the cursor
put at the start of each line for you.

@keys
| `:3,17normal @a` | play `a` once on each of lines 3 to 17 |
| `ea` | append after the end of the word |

@hints
The macro does not need a `j`. `:normal` visits each line of the range
for you and puts the cursor at the start of it.
--
On line 2: `^i'` `Esc`, `ea': { statusCode:` `Esc`, `A, sentry: false },`
`Esc`. Stop recording there.
--
`j`, record, then `:3,17normal @a` and Enter.

@solution
    j  qa ^i' <Esc> ea': { statusCode: <Esc> A, sentry: false }, <Esc> q   :3,17normal @a<Enter>   :wq<Enter>

Sixty-six keystrokes.

Compare this with `15@a`. The count version needs the macro to end
with `j`, and it needs the lines to be consecutive. The range version
needs neither: `:normal` does the stepping, so the macro is purely the
edit, and the range can be anything an Ex command accepts, including
marks, `.` for the current line and `$` for the last. Line numbers are
on the screen; reading two of them is faster than counting sixteen.

There is a second difference. If the macro fails on one line under a
count, everything after it is cancelled. Under `:normal`, a failure on
one line is contained to that line; the next line in the range still
gets its turn. Which of those you want depends on the job. For a
contiguous block of data, either works. For a range that you know
contains lines the macro should skip, `:normal` is the forgiving one,
and the next lesson makes the skipping precise.

@start errors.ts
export const paymentErrors = {
  ORDER_NOT_FOUND 404
  ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED 409
  ORDER_ALREADY_CANCELLED 409
  CUSTOMER_NOT_FOUND 404
  CUSTOMER_SUSPENDED 403
  PAYMENT_DECLINED 402
  PAYMENT_METHOD_EXPIRED 402
  PAYMENT_GATEWAY_TIMEOUT 504
  REFUND_FAILED 502
  REFUND_WINDOW_EXPIRED 409
  REFUND_ALREADY_ISSUED 409
  SHIPMENT_NOT_FOUND 404
  SHIPMENT_ADDRESS_INVALID 422
  INVOICE_NOT_FOUND 404
  INVOICE_ALREADY_PAID 409
  RATE_LIMITED 429
};

